---
// Site-wide "Quick Access" rail: a compact vertical dock on the left edge so a
// visitor anywhere on the site can jump straight to any DFIR_GROUPS,
// IT_GROUPS, or DRILLS_LINKS destination without detouring through the nav.
//
// SELF-EXPLANATION (railHeader below): a first-time visitor has no reason to
// know what this floating panel is or where its links come from — it used to
// open cold on a bare icon switch with no label at all, which read as an
// unstyled, out-of-place box rather than a deliberate feature. `railHeader`
// fixes that directly in the markup (not a dismissible tooltip that can be
// missed once and never seen again): a badge icon, a static "Quick Access"
// title, and a one-line, MODE-AWARE description (`MODE_DESCRIPTIONS` below)
// that names exactly what the current mode surfaces — e.g. "Every DFIR
// reference, tool, and framework on this site." The description updates
// alongside the mode switch's own `render()` call (see the script), so it
// never goes stale after a mode change. A colored top accent border
// (`rail`'s own `borderTop`) plus `railHeader`'s own tinted background give
// the panel visible internal structure instead of one flat slab of
// near-white, which is what made it easy to mistake for a rendering glitch
// at a glance.
//
// A 3-way MODE switch (the first top-toolbar control — a real segmented
// switch, all 3 options always visible at once, not a single icon-swap
// button) picks which dataset the rest of the rail shows — DFIR (Reference /
// Frameworks / Extractors / Converters / Artifact Parsers / Query Builders),
// IT (Reference / Tools), or Drills (the whole flat DRILLS_LINKS array
// wrapped as a single implicit category, see DRILLS_GROUPS below) — never
// more than one at once. All three datasets' category blocks are rendered
// server-side (so a no-JS visitor still gets real, fully labeled links for
// whichever mode the current page belongs to — see `defaultMode` below); JS
// only ever toggles which blocks are hidden, filtered by mode alone (see
// `applyGroupVisibility()`). Desktop/tablet only (`md` and up) — on narrow
// viewports the nav's own <details> accordion already covers this, and a
// fixed-left rail would just overlap body copy.
//
// Two view states: hidden (panel slid fully off-screen, only the edge handle
// remains) and shown (the default — every category of the active mode,
// always labeled, always all at once). There is deliberately no third
// "icons-only" or "single category" state and no separate "show all"
// toggle — every category of whichever mode is active is ALWAYS visible,
// every link is ALWAYS labeled. This used to be optional (an "icons" default
// plus a manually-toggled "show all"/"expand" pair), gated behind a JS
// height check that disabled the all-categories view outright on a short
// window ("unavailable — window too short") since an unbounded stack of 20
// links centered via `top: 50%` on a short viewport had no scroll affordance
// to reach the overflow. That measure-and-disable approach is gone; see the
// scroll-region comment below for what replaced it and why it needed no
// disable path at all.
//
// The hide/show handle toggles hidden ⟷ shown — a small edge tab while
// hidden, docks flush at whichever edge is currently visible (left: 0/236px)
// and grows to the rail's full height once shown — all driven, animated, by
// pure CSS sibling selectors reading `rail`'s own data-view, no JS position
// math — see `handle`'s comment below.
//
// Because every category of a mode (DFIR has 6, 20 links total) is always
// on screen at once, the rail's own toolbar row (drag handle + mode switch)
// stays fixed while everything below it — the actual category/link list —
// scrolls in its own internal region, capped to whatever vertical room is
// actually available below the sticky header on THIS viewport (see
// `scrollRegion`'s own comment for the exact mechanism: a CSS custom
// property the script keeps in sync, with a generous static CSS fallback for
// the brief pre-JS window and genuine no-JS visitors). This is what "an
// effective mechanism to work on all types of clients" means here: a plain
// `max-height` + `overflow-y: auto` adapts to literally any viewport height
// — a half-height split window, a short laptop screen, a tall external
// monitor, a foldable — with no JS-measured "does it fit" branch to get
// wrong, and unlike the old disable-the-feature fallback, content that
// doesn't fit scrolls into view instead of simply vanishing behind a grayed-
// out button.
//
// Per-category color coding: a distinct hue-rotation degree per group index,
// evenly spaced around the color wheel — computed separately per dataset
// since IT_GROUPS' 2 categories need a different spacing than DFIR_GROUPS'
// 6. Applied via CSS `filter: hue-rotate()` (inline `style`, since the exact
// degree is a per-item computed value Panda's static token system can't
// generate a class for) to small, isolated, childless decorative elements
// only — `catDot`/`catAccentBar` below, never `catBlock` itself, since
// `filter` recolors an element's entire rendered subtree and would also
// shift every icon/label color inside it. Rotating a single theme token
// (`primary`) this way — rather than hardcoding fixed colors — is what keeps
// this automatically distinct-but-harmonious across all 10 of the site's
// palettes: each rotation shifts relative to whatever that theme's actual
// hue already is, instead of clashing with half of them. Every category is
// always on screen at once now, so this coloring is purely static, computed
// once server-side per category — no JS ever recolors anything at runtime
// (there's no more "currently active category" concept to track).
//
// State (hidden + mode + vertical drag position) persists via localStorage,
// same "session resuming" pattern as the original tool-only rail this
// replaces.
//
// Every toggle button here is debounced (see `debounceClick` in the script
// below) — CLAUDE.md invariant 12 makes this a sitewide rule for any button
// that drives a CSS/JS transition, not just a fix local to this file.
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import { DFIR_GROUPS, IT_GROUPS, DRILLS_LINKS } from '../consts';

const currentPath = Astro.url.pathname;

// Drills wraps its whole flat link list as a single synthetic category — this
// file only ever reads a group's `category`/`links`, so a bare {category,
// links} pair is enough (no id/blurb/intro — those are /dfir//it/ landing-
// page-only fields, unused here).
const DRILLS_GROUPS = [{ category: 'Drills', links: DRILLS_LINKS }] as const;

// Smart default: open on whichever MODE contains the current page, so a
// first-time visitor lands on a relevant list instead of always DFIR. DFIR
// is the fallback regardless of whether the current page is actually one of
// its own destinations — matching DFIR's own "first/primary" position in
// the nav.
const itHasPage = IT_GROUPS.some((g) => g.links.some((l) => l.href === currentPath));
const drillsHasPage = DRILLS_LINKS.some((l) => l.href === currentPath);
const defaultMode = drillsHasPage ? 'drills' : itHasPage ? 'it' : 'dfir';

const DFIR_HUES = DFIR_GROUPS.map((_, i) => Math.round((360 / DFIR_GROUPS.length) * i));
const IT_HUES = IT_GROUPS.map((_, i) => Math.round((360 / IT_GROUPS.length) * i));
// A single-entry hue array is just [0] (no rotation) — there's nothing to
// color-distinguish with only one category.
const DRILLS_HUES = DRILLS_GROUPS.map((_, i) => Math.round((360 / DRILLS_GROUPS.length) * i));
const HUES_BY_MODE = { dfir: DFIR_HUES, it: IT_HUES, drills: DRILLS_HUES };
const GROUPS_BY_MODE = { dfir: DFIR_GROUPS, it: IT_GROUPS, drills: DRILLS_GROUPS };

// Static — just drives the 3 segment buttons' own icon/label markup below;
// the script keys off each button's own `data-mode-value`, not this array.
const MODE_OPTIONS = [
  { value: 'dfir', label: 'DFIR', icon: 'shield' },
  { value: 'it', label: 'IT', icon: 'wifi' },
  { value: 'drills', label: 'Drills', icon: 'target' },
] as const;
// Server-side mirror of the script's own `modeLabels` (define:vars below) —
// used to compute the hide/show handle's default-mode-aware SSR label so a
// no-JS visitor on a Drills page never sees pre-hydration HTML written for
// the wrong mode (e.g. "Hide DFIR sidebar" on a page actually showing Drills).
const MODE_LABELS = { dfir: 'DFIR', it: 'IT', drills: 'Drills' } as const;

// The one-line, mode-aware explanation `railHeader` shows below the "Quick
// Access" title — see this file's own top comment for why this exists as
// always-visible markup rather than a dismissible onboarding tooltip. Kept
// here (not derived from DFIR_GROUPS/IT_GROUPS) since those blurbs are
// per-category, not per-mode, and this needs one short sentence describing
// the whole dataset a mode surfaces.
const MODE_DESCRIPTIONS = {
  dfir: 'Every DFIR reference, tool, and framework on this site.',
  it: 'General IT & networking reference and tools.',
  drills: "Practice quizzes built from this site's own real data.",
} as const;

const outer = css({
  position: 'fixed',
  left: 0,
  top: '50%',
  zIndex: 45,
  display: { base: 'none', md: 'block' },
});

// Shared between `rail`'s own width and `handle`'s `left` docking offset
// below, so the two can never drift out of sync the way a pair of
// hand-duplicated magic numbers eventually would. Measured, not guessed: the
// longest label ("MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map") is ~178px wide in the real
// Redaction font at itemLabel's 0.78rem (measured via opentype.js against
// the actual .otf — see git history), plus icon/gap/padding/rail-padding —
// sized so every label fits on one line with no ellipsis and no wrap,
// without ballooning the rail's width. There is only ONE non-hidden width
// now (no icons-only state to size separately).
const RAIL_W = '236px';

// `position: relative` purely so `handle` (now `position: absolute`, see
// below) has something to dock against — `rail` itself stays in normal flow.
const slideRow = css({
  position: 'relative',
  transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
});

// The hide/show slide is a transform on `rail` itself (translateX(-100%),
// percentage-based — not a hardcoded px value) so it fully hides regardless
// of the rail's width. Note `transform` doesn't shrink a box's LAYOUT size,
// only where it paints — `rail` still occupies its full width in the
// flex/box model while hidden, which is fine (nothing else needs its layout
// space back) and is exactly why `handle` is docked via explicit sibling
// selectors keyed off `data-view` rather than plain flex-sibling reflow (see
// `handle`'s own comment).
// `borderTop` is a 3px `primary` accent rather than the default 1px border
// color — a deliberate identity marker (see this file's own top comment on
// `railHeader`) so the panel reads as a designed widget at a glance instead
// of a flat, unstyled rectangle.
const rail = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  alignItems: 'stretch',
  gap: '0.08rem',
  w: RAIL_W,
  p: '0.35rem',
  bg: 'bgCard',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  borderLeft: 'none',
  borderTop: '3px solid token(colors.primary)',
  borderTopRightRadius: 'lg',
  borderBottomRightRadius: 'lg',
  boxShadow: 'cardHover',
  overflow: 'hidden',
  transition: 'transform 400ms ease',
  '&[data-view="hidden"]': { transform: 'translateX(-100%)' },
});

// Grab handle — the rail's own first child (in normal flow, not absolutely
// positioned like `handle` below), so it automatically spans the rail's
// current width. Dragging it repositions the whole rail vertically (see the
// script's drag/keyboard/reclamp logic) — `visibility: hidden` (not
// `display: none`) while `data-view="hidden"` both hides it and removes it
// from the tab order (per spec, a `visibility: hidden` element is never
// focusable), matching the rest of the rail translating off-screen in that
// state. `touchAction: none` stops the browser's own touch-scroll gesture
// from competing with a touch drag on this element.
const dragHandle = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  w: '100%',
  h: '14px',
  mb: '0.3rem',
  borderRadius: 'sm',
  color: 'textMuted',
  cursor: 'grab',
  touchAction: 'none',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '&:active': { cursor: 'grabbing' },
  '[data-view="hidden"] &': { visibility: 'hidden' },
});

// The self-explaining header — see this file's own top comment. `bg:
// 'bgSubtle'` (a different tone than `rail`'s own `bgCard`) is what gives the
// panel internal visual layering instead of one flat slab of color.
const railHeader = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'flex-start',
  gap: '0.5rem',
  px: '0.4rem',
  py: '0.45rem',
  mb: '0.3rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  bg: 'bgSubtle',
});

const railHeaderIcon = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  w: '26px',
  h: '26px',
  mt: '0.05rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  bg: 'primary',
  color: 'bg',
  flexShrink: 0,
});

const railHeaderText = css({ minW: 0 });

const railHeaderTitle = css({
  fontSize: '0.8rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  color: 'text',
  lineHeight: 1.2,
});

// Deliberately allowed to WRAP (unlike `itemLabel` below, which must never
// wrap) — this is a short descriptive sentence, not a nav destination label.
const railHeaderDesc = css({
  fontSize: '0.68rem',
  color: 'textMuted',
  lineHeight: 1.35,
  mt: '0.15rem',
});

// Top-toolbar dataset switch — a real segmented switch (role="radiogroup" of
// 3 role="radio" buttons, DFIR / IT / Drills, all three always rendered at
// once — see this file's own top comment) rather than a single icon-swap
// button. Wired into the shared, site-wide sliding-thumb system
// (`src/scripts/animatedSwitch.ts`, mounted once via AnimatedSwitch.astro in
// BaseLayout — see CLAUDE.md invariant 12's "Retrofitting a new switch needs
// exactly one change" note): the track just needs `position: 'relative',
// zIndex: 0` and each button needs `role="radio"` + a `data-active`
// attribute on whichever one is current — no per-component thumb code here
// at all, that script auto-wires every `[role="radiogroup"]` on the page.
//
// Deliberately kept VERTICAL (a stacked column) rather than a horizontal
// row: `animatedSwitch.ts` reads a group's `aria-orientation` ONCE, at
// setup, to decide whether the thumb slides via translateX/width
// (horizontal) or translateY/height (vertical), and never re-reads it
// afterward. There's no longer a narrower "icons" width this rail ever
// shrinks to (the rail has exactly one non-hidden width now), so staying
// vertical is now simply this control's own fixed layout choice, not a
// workaround for a width that used to change out from under it.
const modeSwitchTrack = css({
  position: 'relative', // containing block + stacking context for the animated switch thumb
  zIndex: 0,
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.15rem',
  mb: '0.15rem',
});

// Each segment — icon + label, both always visible now (there's no icons-
// only rail width left to gate the label behind). `w: '100%'` matches
// `item`'s own explicit-percentage convention below. The `&[data-active]`
// flat-color treatment is the same JS-free fallback pattern `ModeToggle`'s
// `segBtn` uses — plain server-rendered CSS, fully correct and accessible
// with zero JS — and gets its own background/border/shadow suppressed
// automatically by the shared `[data-switch-armed] [role='radio'][data-active]`
// rule in motion.css once a thumb is actually armed (see animatedSwitch.ts's
// own header comment); the active-state `color` here is untouched by that
// rule and keeps working as the JS-free indicator no-JS/reduced-motion
// visitors rely on.
const modeSegBtn = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.45rem',
  w: '100%',
  h: '28px',
  px: '0.4rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  border: '1px solid transparent',
  bg: 'transparent',
  color: 'textMuted',
  cursor: 'pointer',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease, border-color 150ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '& svg': { flexShrink: 0 },
  '&[data-active]': {
    bg: 'bgCard',
    color: 'primary',
    borderColor: 'border',
    boxShadow: 'card',
  },
});

const modeSegLabel = css({
  fontSize: '0.72rem',
  fontWeight: 600,
  whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
  minW: 0,
});

const divider = css({ borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.border)', my: '0.3rem' });

// Fixed-height toolbar strip (drag handle, mode switch, divider) that never
// scrolls — only the category/link list below it does. `flexShrink: 0`
// keeps it from ever being compressed by the flex column it sits in
// alongside `scrollRegion`. Measured directly by the script
// (`data-tool-sidebar-toolbar`) to compute how much vertical room is left
// for the scrollable region below, rather than hardcoding a guess that would
// silently drift the moment a control here is added, removed, or resized.
const toolbar = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  flexShrink: 0,
});

// The scrollable region holding every category block — see this file's own
// top comment for the full "why" behind replacing the old JS-measured
// show-all-or-disable mechanism with this. `maxHeight` reads a CSS custom
// property (`--sidebar-max-h`) the script keeps in sync with the real space
// available below the sticky header on THIS viewport (`updateScrollCap()`
// below), falling back to a generous static value for the brief pre-JS
// window and for genuine no-JS visitors — the fallback is deliberately
// generous rather than tight, since a no-JS visitor gets no refinement pass
// afterward. `overscrollBehavior: 'contain'` stops a scroll that reaches the
// top/bottom of this list from chaining into a scroll of the page body
// behind it. `pr` gives the native scrollbar (when one appears) a sliver of
// breathing room instead of sitting flush against link text.
const scrollRegion = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.08rem',
  overflowY: 'auto',
  overflowX: 'hidden',
  overscrollBehavior: 'contain',
  maxHeight: 'var(--sidebar-max-h, calc(100dvh - 220px))',
  pr: '0.15rem',
});

// Wraps one category's header + link list together so a single `hidden`
// toggle controls both atomically (see the script's `applyGroupVisibility`)
// — the alternative, syncing two separately-hidden siblings, is one more
// place for the header and its links to drift out of sync.
const catBlock = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.1rem',
  // Anchors `catAccentBar` below (position:absolute) to this block, not the
  // whole rail — so each category's colored rail spans just its own header
  // + links, not the full stack. `pl` makes room for it sitting flush at
  // this block's own left edge.
  position: 'relative',
  pl: '0.5rem',
  '&[hidden]': { display: 'none' },
});

// Colored left-rail marking each category's own section — a separate,
// isolated, childless element rather than a border directly on `catBlock`
// itself: `filter: hue-rotate()` (applied inline per-category, see
// HUES_BY_MODE) recolors an element's ENTIRE rendered subtree, so putting it
// on `catBlock` would also shift every link icon/label inside — putting it
// on this empty decorative bar instead means only the bar itself gets
// recolored.
const catAccentBar = css({
  display: 'block',
  position: 'absolute',
  left: 0,
  top: '0.1rem',
  bottom: '0.05rem',
  w: '2px',
  borderRadius: 'full',
  bg: 'primary',
});

const navGroup = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.05rem',
});

// Always visible now — every category is always on screen, so a category
// label is never optional the way it used to be behind an "expanded" toggle.
const catHeader = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.4rem',
  fontSize: '0.68rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  textTransform: 'uppercase',
  letterSpacing: '0.05em',
  color: 'textMuted',
  whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
  m: 0,
  mt: '0.3rem',
});

// Small solid color-coded dot — the per-category identity marker next to
// each `catHeader`'s own label. `boxShadow` here (not a separate glow
// element) picks up the same `filter: hue-rotate()` applied inline
// per-instance, since filter recolors an element's whole rendered output
// including its own shadow — a soft glow in the dot's own color for free, no
// extra markup.
const catDot = css({
  display: 'inline-block',
  w: '7px',
  h: '7px',
  borderRadius: 'full',
  bg: 'primary',
  boxShadow: '0 0 4px 0 token(colors.primary)',
  flexShrink: 0,
});

const item = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.45rem',
  w: '100%',
  h: '28px',
  px: '0.4rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  color: 'textMuted',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'text', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '&[aria-current="page"]': { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '& svg': { flexShrink: 0 },
});

// Wraps each link's own <Icon>, carrying the same per-category
// `hue-rotate()` inline style as `catDot`/`catAccentBar` (see HUES_BY_MODE).
// Deliberately NOT applied to `item` itself — same reasoning as
// `catAccentBar`'s own comment: `filter` recolors an element's whole
// rendered subtree, and `item` also contains the label text, which must
// stay in `item`'s own resting/hover/active color, not get hue-shifted. The
// effect is quiet by design rather than a second, competing color system:
// `currentColor` at rest is `item`'s muted `textMuted`, which is
// low-saturation enough that rotating its hue is barely perceptible — the
// tint only really reads once `item` reaches its `[aria-current="page"]`
// state, where `color: primary` is a fully saturated theme color and the
// same rotation becomes clearly visible.
const itemIcon = css({ display: 'inline-flex', flexShrink: 0 });

// Always visible now — no icons-only rail width left to gate this behind.
// Kept in the DOM always (rather than JS-inserted) so a screen reader
// always has real link text, not just aria-label. No truncation: `RAIL_W`
// is sized (see its own comment) so every label — including the longest,
// "MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map" — fits on this single nowrap line with no
// ellipsis. Never add overflow/textOverflow here; widen RAIL_W instead if a
// longer label is ever added.
const itemLabel = css({
  fontSize: '0.78rem',
  whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
  minW: 0,
});

// `position: absolute` (docked against `slideRow`, not flex-adjacent to
// `rail`) is deliberate: `left` and `height` are driven by two EXPLICIT,
// mutually-exclusive rules keyed off `rail`'s own `data-view` attribute (the
// `[data-tool-sidebar-rail][data-view="…"] ~ &` sibling selectors below) —
// one per real state — rather than inferring position from rail's live
// flex-box width the way a plain flex sibling would. That's the fix for the
// handle landing in the wrong spot: `rail`'s hide is a `transform`, which
// repositions where it PAINTS but doesn't shrink its LAYOUT width (see
// `rail`'s comment), so a flex-sibling handle used to dock wherever rail's
// width happened to be *before* it was last hidden, instead of always
// sitting flush at the true visible edge.
//
// Small (32px) by default/hidden — just tall enough to be a comfortable tap
// target — and grows to the rail's full height whenever the rail is
// actually showing, animated via the same sibling-selector mechanism.
// `height: 100%` resolves against `slideRow`'s height, which (`handle` is
// out of normal flow) is dictated purely by `rail`'s own intrinsic content
// height, so this tracks correctly as the link list's own scroll-capped
// height changes.
const handle = css({
  position: 'absolute',
  top: 0,
  left: RAIL_W,
  display: 'inline-flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  w: '22px',
  h: '32px',
  bg: 'bgCard',
  color: 'textMuted',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  borderLeft: 'none',
  borderTopRightRadius: 'md',
  borderBottomRightRadius: 'md',
  cursor: 'pointer',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, left 300ms ease, height 350ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'primary' },
  // `var(--rail-h, …)`, not a literal `100%`: `handle` is `position: absolute`
  // against `slideRow`, whose own height is "auto" (derived purely from
  // `rail`'s in-flow content) — per spec, a percentage `height` on an
  // absolutely-positioned element only resolves against a containing block
  // with a DEFINITE (non-auto) height, which slideRow doesn't have. `100%`
  // here would silently behave as `auto` and shrink to the button's own
  // intrinsic content size instead of matching rail. `--rail-h` is a real
  // measured pixel value the script below keeps in sync with `rail`'s live
  // height via ResizeObserver, and `350ms` is enough for `transition: height`
  // to still animate smoothly between two concrete px values.
  '[data-tool-sidebar-rail]:not([data-view="hidden"]) ~ &': { h: 'var(--rail-h, 100%)' },
  '[data-tool-sidebar-rail][data-view="hidden"] ~ &': { left: 0, h: '32px' },
  // Explicit rotate(0deg) base (not just the absence of a transform rule) so
  // both endpoints of the toggle are concrete values for the transition to
  // interpolate between, rather than relying on an implicit "no transform".
  '& .tool-sidebar-chev': { transition: 'transform 500ms ease', transform: 'rotate(0deg)' },
  '&[aria-expanded="true"] .tool-sidebar-chev': { transform: 'rotate(180deg)' },
});
---

<div class={outer} data-tool-sidebar>
  <div class={slideRow}>
    <div class={`tool-sidebar-slide ${rail}`} data-tool-sidebar-rail data-view="shown">
      <div class={toolbar} data-tool-sidebar-toolbar>
        <button
          type="button"
          class={dragHandle}
          data-tool-sidebar-drag
          aria-label="Drag to move the sidebar up or down. Arrow keys nudge it; Home or a double-click recenters it."
          title="Drag to reposition · double-click to recenter"
        >
          <Icon name="grip-horizontal" size={14} />
        </button>

        <div class={railHeader}>
          <span class={railHeaderIcon} aria-hidden="true">
            <Icon name="grid" size={15} />
          </span>
          <div class={railHeaderText}>
            <p class={railHeaderTitle}>Quick Access</p>
            <p class={railHeaderDesc} data-tool-sidebar-desc>{MODE_DESCRIPTIONS[defaultMode]}</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class={modeSwitchTrack} role="radiogroup" aria-label="Sidebar dataset" aria-orientation="vertical" data-tool-sidebar-mode-toggle>
          {
            MODE_OPTIONS.map((opt) => (
              <button
                type="button"
                role="radio"
                class={modeSegBtn}
                data-tool-sidebar-mode-btn
                data-mode-value={opt.value}
                aria-checked={defaultMode === opt.value ? 'true' : 'false'}
                aria-label={opt.label}
                title={opt.label}
                {...(defaultMode === opt.value ? { 'data-active': '' } : {})}
              >
                <Icon name={opt.icon} size={15} />
                <span class={modeSegLabel}>{opt.label}</span>
              </button>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class={divider} />
      </div>

      <div class={scrollRegion} data-tool-sidebar-scroll>
        {
          (['dfir', 'it', 'drills'] as const).map((m) =>
            GROUPS_BY_MODE[m].map((group, i) => (
              <div class={catBlock} data-tool-sidebar-mode={m} hidden={m !== defaultMode}>
                <span class={catAccentBar} aria-hidden="true" style={{ filter: `hue-rotate(${HUES_BY_MODE[m][i]}deg)` }} />
                <p class={catHeader} role="heading" aria-level="3">
                  <span class={catDot} aria-hidden="true" style={{ filter: `hue-rotate(${HUES_BY_MODE[m][i]}deg)` }} />
                  {group.category}
                </p>
                <nav class={navGroup} aria-label={group.category}>
                  {group.links.map((link) => (
                    <a
                      href={link.href}
                      class={item}
                      aria-label={link.label}
                      title={link.label}
                      aria-current={currentPath === link.href ? 'page' : undefined}
                    >
                      <span class={itemIcon} style={{ filter: `hue-rotate(${HUES_BY_MODE[m][i]}deg)` }}>
                        <Icon name={link.icon} size={19} />
                      </span>
                      <span class={itemLabel}>{link.label}</span>
                    </a>
                  ))}
                </nav>
              </div>
            ))
          )
        }
      </div>
    </div>
    <button
      type="button"
      class={`tool-sidebar-handle ${handle}`}
      data-tool-sidebar-toggle
      aria-expanded="true"
      aria-label={`Hide ${MODE_LABELS[defaultMode]} sidebar`}
      title={`Hide ${MODE_LABELS[defaultMode]} sidebar`}
    >
      <Icon name="chevron-right" size={13} class="tool-sidebar-chev" />
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<script
  is:inline
  define:vars={{
    modeLabels: MODE_LABELS,
    modeDescriptions: MODE_DESCRIPTIONS,
    defaultMode,
  }}
>
  // @ts-nocheck — vanilla DOM client script
  (function () {
    var root = document.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar]');
    if (!root) return;
    var rail = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-rail]');
    var hideToggle = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-toggle]');
    var toolbarEl = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-toolbar]');
    var scrollRegionEl = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-scroll]');
    var descEl = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-desc]');
    // The 3 mode segment buttons (DFIR/IT/Drills) — `data-tool-sidebar-mode-toggle`
    // itself marks the group/track (see markup). animatedSwitch.ts finds it
    // generically via `[role="radiogroup"]` for the sliding thumb, so this
    // script never needs it for that; it does need its own reference below
    // to wire the arrow-key navigation the radiogroup role advertises to
    // assistive tech (native `role="radio"` buttons carry none of that for
    // free — see the keydown listener further down).
    var modeSegBtns = Array.prototype.slice.call(root.querySelectorAll('[data-tool-sidebar-mode-btn]'));
    var modeSwitchTrackEl = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-mode-toggle]');
    var dragHandleEl = root.querySelector('[data-tool-sidebar-drag]');
    // Every category block, across all 3 modes — each carries its own
    // data-tool-sidebar-mode, so a single NodeList + a mode check in
    // applyGroupVisibility() covers filtering by mode in one pass.
    var groups = root.querySelectorAll('[data-tool-sidebar-mode]');
    if (!rail || !hideToggle) return;

    // The sticky nav `<header>` (z-index 50) paints above `rail` (z-index 45,
    // see `outer`'s own comment) — factored into both the drag bounds and the
    // scroll-region height cap below, so neither lets the rail's own content
    // end up covered by (or clipped under) the header.
    var headerEl = document.querySelector('header');

    var HIDDEN_KEY = 'tool-sidebar-hidden';
    var MODE_KEY = 'tool-sidebar-mode';
    var POSITION_KEY = 'tool-sidebar-y';

    var hidden = false;
    // Vertical drag position — see the drag-handle block near the bottom of
    // this script for the full explanation. `positioned` is false until the
    // user has dragged (or a stored position was restored), meaning the rail
    // is still on the CSS-only default (`outer`'s `top: 50%` + `slideRow`'s
    // `translateY(-50%)`, centered with zero JS involvement — the safe no-JS
    // fallback). `centerY` (viewport px) is only meaningful once positioned.
    var positioned = false;
    var centerY = 0;
    // Mode (dfir/it/drills) picks which dataset is shown.
    var mode = defaultMode;

    // The rail's true, currently-PAINTED vertical center — reads off
    // `rail.getBoundingClientRect()` rather than trusting `centerY`/CSS math,
    // since `getBoundingClientRect()` reflects the real rendered position
    // (post-transform) regardless of whether the rail is still on the CSS
    // default (`top: 50%` + `translateY(-50%)`) or already `positioned` via
    // JS. Used to seed a drag/keyboard move from wherever the rail visually
    // is right now, so the very first drag never jumps.
    function currentCenterY() {
      if (positioned) return centerY;
      var r = rail.getBoundingClientRect();
      return r.top + r.height / 2;
    }

    // Vertical room the rail is allowed to occupy: below the sticky nav
    // header and above the viewport's own bottom edge, each with a small
    // breathing-room margin. Measured fresh every call — like every other
    // layout read in this file, never cached — since both the header's
    // height (ticker) and the rail's own height (mode-dependent, and now
    // also scroll-capped) can change between calls.
    function dragBounds() {
      var h = rail.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      var margin = 8;
      var minCenterY = (headerEl ? headerEl.getBoundingClientRect().bottom : 0) + margin + h / 2;
      var maxCenterY = window.innerHeight - margin - h / 2;
      if (minCenterY > maxCenterY) {
        // The rail is taller than the viewport has room for even flush
        // against the header — split the difference rather than forcing an
        // arbitrary edge. In practice this should be rare now that
        // scrollRegion is height-capped, but stays as a graceful fallback.
        var mid = (minCenterY + maxCenterY) / 2;
        return { min: mid, max: mid };
      }
      return { min: minCenterY, max: maxCenterY };
    }

    function clampCenterY(y) {
      var b = dragBounds();
      return Math.min(b.max, Math.max(b.min, y));
    }

    // The only place that writes `outer`'s (== `root`'s) inline `top` — a
    // no-op until `positioned` is true, so a visitor who never drags the
    // handle never gets an inline style at all and stays on the pure-CSS
    // centered default.
    function applyPosition() {
      if (!positioned) return;
      root.style.top = centerY + 'px';
    }

    // Re-clamps the CURRENT position against the CURRENT viewport/rail size
    // — called after every render() (a hide/show or mode change can change
    // the rail's own height) and on window resize, so a position that was
    // valid a moment ago can never end up pushing the rail off-screen once
    // its height or the viewport changes. A no-op while un-positioned, same
    // reasoning as applyPosition().
    function reclampPosition() {
      if (!positioned) return;
      centerY = clampCenterY(centerY);
      applyPosition();
    }

    // Sets a new position, always through the same clamp — used by both the
    // live pointer-drag (persist=false, called on every pointermove — far
    // too often to hit localStorage each time) and the keyboard nudge/reset
    // paths (persist=true, called once per discrete keypress).
    function setCenterY(y, persist) {
      centerY = clampCenterY(y);
      positioned = true;
      applyPosition();
      if (persist) {
        try {
          localStorage.setItem(POSITION_KEY, String(centerY / window.innerHeight));
        } catch (e) {}
      }
    }

    // Clears any custom position and lets the CSS default (centered) take
    // back over — the drag handle's "undo" (double-click or Home key).
    function resetPosition() {
      positioned = false;
      root.style.top = '';
      try {
        localStorage.removeItem(POSITION_KEY);
      } catch (e) {}
    }

    // Wraps a discrete, JS-driven position change (keyboard nudge/reset —
    // NOT the live pointer-drag, which sets position on every pointermove
    // and would look laggy chasing a transition) in a short `top` transition
    // so it settles smoothly instead of snapping. Tracked so overlapping
    // calls (e.g. OS key-repeat firing keydown faster than the 170ms settle
    // window) cancel the previous cleanup instead of racing it.
    var topTransitionTimer = null;
    function withTopTransition(fn) {
      clearTimeout(topTransitionTimer);
      root.style.transition = 'top 150ms ease';
      fn();
      topTransitionTimer = setTimeout(function () {
        root.style.transition = '';
        topTransitionTimer = null;
      }, 170);
    }

    function render() {
      rail.setAttribute('data-view', hidden ? 'hidden' : 'shown');
      var modeLabel = modeLabels[mode];
      hideToggle.setAttribute('aria-expanded', hidden ? 'false' : 'true');
      hideToggle.setAttribute('aria-label', hidden ? 'Show ' + modeLabel + ' sidebar' : 'Hide ' + modeLabel + ' sidebar');
      hideToggle.setAttribute('title', hidden ? 'Show ' + modeLabel + ' sidebar' : 'Hide ' + modeLabel + ' sidebar');
      if (descEl) descEl.textContent = modeDescriptions[mode];
      // Keeps <main>'s own reserved left margin (BaseLayout.astro, see its
      // own comment) in sync with whether the rail is actually occupying
      // that space — a visitor who's hidden the rail gets the full
      // originally-centered column back, not a permanent empty gutter.
      // Same "script keeps a CSS custom property in sync" pattern this file
      // already uses for --sidebar-max-h/--rail-h below.
      document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--sidebar-reserve', hidden ? '0px' : '236px');
      // A hide/show or mode change can change the rail's own height —
      // re-clamp a custom drag position against the new height so it never
      // ends up pushing the rail off-screen (see reclampPosition's own
      // comment). A no-op while the rail is still on the CSS-centered
      // default.
      reclampPosition();
    }

    // The one place that decides which category blocks are actually
    // hidden — a block only ever shows in its own mode. Called after
    // anything that changes `mode`; nothing else should ever set a group's
    // `.hidden` directly.
    function applyGroupVisibility() {
      for (var i = 0; i < groups.length; i++) {
        var g = groups[i];
        g.hidden = g.getAttribute('data-tool-sidebar-mode') !== mode;
      }
    }

    // Keeps `scrollRegion`'s `--sidebar-max-h` custom property in sync with
    // the real vertical room available below the sticky header on THIS
    // viewport — see `scrollRegion`'s own CSS comment for why this exists.
    // Total top+bottom breathing room, not per-edge: `rail` is vertically
    // centered (`top: 50%`), so reserving enough room to clear a
    // `headerBottom`-tall obstruction at the top requires *double* that
    // amount of total margin (the other half goes unused at the bottom,
    // which is harmless — nothing opaque sits there). `toolbarEl`'s own
    // height is measured live (not hardcoded) so a future toolbar change
    // (adding/removing a control) can't silently throw this off. Called on
    // load and on a debounced resize — never cached, same reasoning as any
    // other layout read in this file (the self-hosted font's `font-display:
    // swap` can change layout height between an early read and now).
    function updateScrollCap() {
      if (!scrollRegionEl) return;
      var headerMargin = headerEl ? headerEl.getBoundingClientRect().bottom * 2 : 0;
      var viewportMargin = Math.max(64, headerMargin);
      var toolbarH = toolbarEl ? toolbarEl.getBoundingClientRect().height : 0;
      var buffer = 24; // rail's own padding + a little breathing room — not measured to the pixel, unused whitespace here is harmless
      var available = window.innerHeight - viewportMargin - toolbarH - buffer;
      scrollRegionEl.style.setProperty('--sidebar-max-h', Math.max(120, available) + 'px');
    }

    try {
      hidden = localStorage.getItem(HIDDEN_KEY) === '1';
      var storedMode = localStorage.getItem(MODE_KEY);
      if (storedMode === 'dfir' || storedMode === 'it' || storedMode === 'drills') mode = storedMode;
      // Stored as a FRACTION of viewport height (0–1), not a raw px value —
      // a raw px position from a previous, differently-sized viewport
      // (an external monitor vs a laptop screen, say) wouldn't mean the same
      // thing on this one; a fraction re-scales sensibly and still gets
      // clamped against the real bounds below regardless. Restoring this
      // here (before render() runs below) means the very first render()'s
      // own reclampPosition() call already has the right
      // `positioned`/`centerY` to work with — no separate apply step needed.
      var storedFraction = localStorage.getItem(POSITION_KEY);
      if (storedFraction !== null) {
        var f = Number(storedFraction);
        if (!isNaN(f)) {
          centerY = f * window.innerHeight;
          positioned = true;
        }
      }
    } catch (e) {}

    // Single source of truth for which of the 3 mode segment buttons is
    // marked active — used both here (restoring from localStorage, which can
    // disagree with the server-rendered defaultMode) and from each button's
    // own click handler below. Setting `data-active` is also what
    // animatedSwitch.ts's MutationObserver picks up to (re)place the sliding
    // thumb — see modeSwitchTrack's own comment.
    function syncModeButtons() {
      modeSegBtns.forEach(function (b) {
        var active = b.getAttribute('data-mode-value') === mode;
        if (active) b.setAttribute('data-active', '');
        else b.removeAttribute('data-active');
        b.setAttribute('aria-checked', active ? 'true' : 'false');
      });
    }
    syncModeButtons();
    applyGroupVisibility();
    render();
    updateScrollCap();

    var resizeTimer = null;
    window.addEventListener('resize', function () {
      clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
      resizeTimer = setTimeout(function () {
        updateScrollCap();
        reclampPosition();
      }, 150);
    });

    // `handle` is `position: absolute` and reads this custom property for its
    // own height (see `handle`'s CSS comment for why a plain `height: 100%`
    // can't work here) — keep it in sync with rail's REAL measured height,
    // not a guess. ResizeObserver re-fires on every layout change (mode
    // switch, font load, viewport resize, scroll-cap change), not just once
    // on load.
    function syncHandleHeight() {
      var h = rail.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      if (h > 0) hideToggle.style.setProperty('--rail-h', h + 'px');
    }
    syncHandleHeight();
    if (typeof ResizeObserver !== 'undefined') {
      new ResizeObserver(syncHandleHeight).observe(rail);
    } else {
      window.addEventListener('resize', syncHandleHeight);
    }

    // Debounce every toggle in this rail: while a button's own cooldown is
    // running, further clicks on it are ignored outright (not queued) — see
    // CLAUDE.md invariant 12 for why this is a sitewide rule, not a one-off.
    // `cooldownMs` should be >= the button's own longest CSS transition, so
    // a second click can never land mid-animation and thrash the state.
    function debounceClick(el, cooldownMs, handler) {
      var busy = false;
      el.addEventListener('click', function () {
        if (busy) return;
        busy = true;
        handler();
        setTimeout(function () {
          busy = false;
        }, cooldownMs);
      });
    }

    debounceClick(hideToggle, 550, function () {
      hidden = !hidden;
      render();
      try {
        localStorage.setItem(HIDDEN_KEY, hidden ? '1' : '0');
      } catch (e) {}
    });

    // Deliberately NOT run through debounceClick, unlike hideToggle above —
    // same documented exception animatedSwitch.ts's own MutationObserver
    // comment makes: that hard rule (CLAUDE.md invariant 12) guards against
    // a rapid SECOND click re-triggering a toggle that fully REVERSES the
    // first. A 3-way switch has no such failure mode — clicking DFIR→IT→
    // Drills in rapid succession is three independent, absolute selections,
    // not a reversal, and motion/mini's thumb naturally redirects mid-slide
    // from wherever it currently is (see animatedSwitch.ts's
    // currentTranslate()). The cheap `value === mode` guard below is about
    // avoiding redundant work on a re-click of the already-active segment,
    // not about debouncing.
    modeSegBtns.forEach(function (btn) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
        var value = btn.getAttribute('data-mode-value');
        if (value === mode) return;
        mode = value;
        syncModeButtons();
        applyGroupVisibility();
        try {
          localStorage.setItem(MODE_KEY, mode);
        } catch (e) {}
        render();
      });
    });

    // Arrow-key navigation for the mode radiogroup — plain `role="radio"`
    // buttons (not real `<input type="radio">`) get zero native arrow-key
    // handling, a gap this control shares with ModeToggle.astro/
    // ViewToggle.astro (pre-existing there, out of scope to fix here).
    // Mirrors ThemePicker.astro's own onKey/moveFocus shape. Orientation is
    // permanently vertical (see modeSwitchTrack's own comment), so only
    // ArrowUp/ArrowDown are wired — Home/End jump to the first/last segment.
    // Per the WAI-ARIA radiogroup pattern ("selection follows focus"), moving
    // focus also selects: each move calls `.click()` on the newly focused
    // button rather than duplicating the click handler's own mode-switch
    // logic, so there's exactly one place that decides what activating a
    // segment does.
    if (modeSwitchTrackEl) {
      modeSwitchTrackEl.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
        var idx = modeSegBtns.indexOf(document.activeElement);
        if (idx === -1) return;
        var target = null;
        if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') target = modeSegBtns[(idx + 1) % modeSegBtns.length];
        else if (e.key === 'ArrowUp') target = modeSegBtns[(idx - 1 + modeSegBtns.length) % modeSegBtns.length];
        else if (e.key === 'Home') target = modeSegBtns[0];
        else if (e.key === 'End') target = modeSegBtns[modeSegBtns.length - 1];
        if (!target) return;
        e.preventDefault();
        target.focus();
        target.click();
      });
    }

    // Vertical drag: pointerdown on the grip handle starts a drag, tracked
    // via Pointer Events (unifies mouse/touch/pen — no separate touch
    // handlers needed) and `setPointerCapture` so a fast drag that outruns
    // the handle's own small hit area doesn't drop the gesture. Deliberately
    // NOT run through `debounceClick` — that helper guards against a rapid
    // *second click* re-triggering a whole toggle transition, which doesn't
    // apply here: a continuous drag is one held gesture, not repeated
    // discrete clicks, and it needs every pointermove to land, not be
    // ignored while "busy".
    if (dragHandleEl) {
      var dragPointerId = null;
      var dragStartY = 0;
      var dragStartCenterY = 0;

      function onDragPointerMove(e) {
        if (dragPointerId === null || e.pointerId !== dragPointerId) return;
        setCenterY(dragStartCenterY + (e.clientY - dragStartY), false);
      }

      function endDrag(e) {
        if (dragPointerId === null || (e && e.pointerId !== dragPointerId)) return;
        try {
          dragHandleEl.releasePointerCapture(dragPointerId);
        } catch (err) {}
        dragPointerId = null;
        document.removeEventListener('pointermove', onDragPointerMove);
        document.removeEventListener('pointerup', endDrag);
        document.removeEventListener('pointercancel', endDrag);
        document.body.style.removeProperty('cursor');
        document.body.style.removeProperty('user-select');
        // Persist only once, on release — not on every pointermove, which
        // can fire dozens of times a second and would otherwise hammer
        // localStorage for no benefit (nothing reads the in-progress value
        // until a future page load anyway). Gated on `positioned`, which
        // setCenterY only flips true on a real pointermove: a plain click
        // (pointerdown immediately followed by pointerup, no move in
        // between) must not write centerY's untouched init value of 0 —
        // that would silently jam the rail under the header on the *next*
        // page load, with no visible link back to the stray click that
        // caused it.
        if (positioned) {
          try {
            localStorage.setItem(POSITION_KEY, String(centerY / window.innerHeight));
          } catch (err) {}
        }
      }

      dragHandleEl.addEventListener('pointerdown', function (e) {
        if (dragPointerId !== null) return; // ignore a second pointer while one drag is already active
        if (e.button !== undefined && e.button !== 0) return; // primary button/touch/pen only
        dragPointerId = e.pointerId;
        dragStartY = e.clientY;
        dragStartCenterY = currentCenterY();
        try {
          dragHandleEl.setPointerCapture(dragPointerId);
        } catch (err) {}
        document.addEventListener('pointermove', onDragPointerMove);
        document.addEventListener('pointerup', endDrag);
        document.addEventListener('pointercancel', endDrag);
        // Matches the handle's own `cursor: grabbing` (see dragHandle's CSS)
        // even if the pointer strays outside the handle mid-drag, and stops
        // the rest of the page from selecting text while dragging across it.
        document.body.style.cursor = 'grabbing';
        document.body.style.userSelect = 'none';
        e.preventDefault();
      });

      // Double-click/double-tap recenters — the drag equivalent of every
      // other stateful control on this site having a "back to default" path.
      dragHandleEl.addEventListener('dblclick', function () {
        withTopTransition(resetPosition);
      });

      // Keyboard equivalent for the same move: arrow keys nudge (Shift for a
      // bigger jump), Home recenters. Both animate briefly via
      // withTopTransition — unlike the live drag above, each keypress is one
      // discrete change, so a short settle transition reads as intentional
      // feedback rather than as lag.
      dragHandleEl.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
        if (e.key === 'ArrowUp' || e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
          e.preventDefault();
          var step = e.shiftKey ? 80 : 20;
          var delta = e.key === 'ArrowUp' ? -step : step;
          withTopTransition(function () {
            setCenterY(currentCenterY() + delta, true);
          });
        } else if (e.key === 'Home') {
          e.preventDefault();
          withTopTransition(resetPosition);
        }
      });
    }
  })();
</script>
